1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,339
This is Ghost Bills. Here is a short tour.

2
00:00:03,789 --> 00:00:09,890
Ghost Bills reads your bank statement and finds the subscriptions you forgot about. We call them ghosts.

3
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:16,868
Why? Small subscriptions are easy to forget. A few dollars a month can add up to hundreds of dollars a year.

4
00:00:17,318 --> 00:00:22,545
Everything runs on your own laptop, even the AI. Your bank data never leaves it.

5
00:00:22,995 --> 00:00:27,902
Step one. Download a CSV file from your bank, and click Import statement.

6
00:00:28,702 --> 00:00:34,654
Ghost Bills finds the date, description and amount columns for you. Check them, then click Import.

7
00:00:35,454 --> 00:00:41,022
Step two. Plain code finds charges that repeat, and checks them with four simple rules.

8
00:00:41,472 --> 00:00:48,107
A Poltergeist is a price that went up. Moonbeam Music went from $9.99 to $12.99.

9
00:00:48,557 --> 00:00:52,120
A Sleepy Ghost is a yearly plan that will charge you soon.

10
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:56,133
A Shapeshifter is a free trial that turned into a paid plan.

11
00:00:56,583 --> 00:01:00,018
Twin Ghosts are two subscriptions that do the same job.

12
00:01:00,468 --> 00:01:07,337
At the top, you see what your ghosts cost you each year. Here, $466.76.

13
00:01:07,787 --> 00:01:10,091
Step three. Click a ghost to see the proof.

14
00:01:10,891 --> 00:01:15,563
These numbers come straight from your own statement. Code finds them, not AI.

15
00:01:16,013 --> 00:01:23,693
Gemma, an open AI model running on your laptop, explains why it matters. A checker blocks any number it makes up.

16
00:01:24,143 --> 00:01:27,962
When Ghost Bills is not sure, Penny asks you, instead of guessing.

17
00:01:28,412 --> 00:01:31,911
Step four. Ghost Bills writes a short cancel email for you.

18
00:01:32,361 --> 00:01:38,825
Now the new part. Penny can send that email for you, from your own Gmail. Here is how that stays safe.

19
00:01:39,275 --> 00:01:49,622
Three agents share the work. Penny chats and plans. Ledger reads your statement on this laptop, and never goes online. Courier is the only one allowed to send.

20
00:01:50,072 --> 00:01:59,587
It starts with Auth0. You log in, and Auth0 gives you a token. A token is a signed pass. It says who you are, and what its holder may do.

21
00:02:00,037 --> 00:02:09,509
Penny never uses your pass. She trades it at Auth0 for her own, with token exchange. Her pass says: acting for you, read only, for five minutes.

22
00:02:09,959 --> 00:02:19,090
To send, Courier trades Penny's pass for a send pass. Auth0 adds Courier to the chain, so the pass reads: you, then Penny, then Courier.

23
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:29,204
So there are three locks. Penny can only read. Only Courier's pass reaches the send step. And nothing is sent until you confirm, by logging in again.

24
00:02:29,654 --> 00:02:37,569
Here is how it looks. Type the company's address, and press Ask for approval. Then confirm by logging in again, within two minutes.

25
00:02:38,019 --> 00:02:49,134
After you confirm, Auth0 Token Vault gives Courier a short Google token that can only send email. Gmail sends one email, and the token is thrown away.

26
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:54,960
So the only thing that leaves your laptop is that one email. Your bank data never does.

27
00:02:55,410 --> 00:03:05,159
Who can do what right now shows each agent's token, as readable facts, never the token itself. Courier only holds one for the seconds it takes to send.

28
00:03:05,609 --> 00:03:08,062
Stop all agents ends everything at once.

29
00:03:08,512 --> 00:03:11,499
And What Penny did lists every step, so you can check it.

30
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:16,770
When the company confirms, click I cancelled it, and the ghost moves to the graveyard.

31
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:26,287
Your bank data never leaves your laptop. Only the one email you approved does. Code finds the ghosts. AI explains them. You decide.

32
00:03:26,737 --> 00:03:27,804
Happy haunting!
